WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:05.099
The people out there made it clear they strongly support the American Rescue Plan.

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:16.050
This historic legislation is about rebuilding the backbone of this country and giving people in this nation, working people, middle class folks, people who built the country a fighting chance.

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:24.510
The American Rescue Plan Act provided 19.5 billion dollars in federal aid to municipalities with a population of less than 50,000 people.

00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:31.020
Brady, which has a population of about 5,500, is known as the heart of Texas because it's smack in the middle of the state.

00:00:31.350 --> 00:00:38.219
The federal government offered it $1.3 million, but the City Council voted to turn down the money because there were strings attached.

00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:41.579
Obviously, we could have used the money, but at what cost?

00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:48.899
It stated that recipients shall comply with all federal statutes, regulations and executive orders.

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:53.759
Sheila Hemphill is a lobbyist and the founder of the Citizens Advocacy Group 

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:54.319
Texas Right to Know.

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:57.540
We live where we live because we like knowing who our neighbors are.

00:00:57.570 --> 00:00:59.039
We like helping our neighbors.

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:04.170
We like having the ability to influence the laws in which we're under at a local level

00:01:04.379 --> 00:01:10.019
 it's not worth being subservient to executive orders, especially when they're unknown

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:17.249
Brady was one of at least 243 small towns across the country to reject or return the federal stimulus aid.

00:01:17.550 --> 00:01:23.190
Residents were concerned about a clause in the contract, giving the federal government the right to audit how the funds were used.

00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:29.250
They were also worried that the money would allow the Biden administration to force the town to comply with its vaccine mandate.

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:37.529
An executive order issued in September of last year mandated that all companies with more than 100 employees require all of their workers to be vaccinated.

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:46.799
Though it was later struck down by the Supreme Court, the order wouldn't have had much of an impact on Brady since its school district is the only entity in town large enough to be affected.

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:51.900
But for the people here, it was about the principle of the matter in asserting their local sovereignty.

00:01:52.080 --> 00:02:03.400
Today it's about the COVID 19 vaccine mandate, but the big problem is it's an executive order that could be who knows what, because that comes with the stroke of a pen and wants Sheila contacted me.

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:05.310
Yes, the red light went off.

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:10.969
Superleggera is an attorney and city commissioner in the neighboring community of Mason, population 2400.

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:15.440
She led the charge to reject five hundred and seventy thousand dollars in stimulus funding.

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:21.270
The federal government has no right to come in and audit us has no right to our financial records.

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:24.060
We haven't entered a contract with them.

00:02:24.270 --> 00:02:27.719
We're separate and that's the way we want to keep it here in Mason.

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:36.990
The contract Brady and Mason would have had to sign to receive the American Rescue Plan Act funding is typical of how Washington uses federal aid to influence state and local policy.

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:41.490
Bribery is a very good term for what the federal government is doing.

00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:47.400
The only way that you can avoid the consequences of being bribed is to not take the bribe

00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:55.781
Mike Maharrey is the national communications director at the Tenth Amendment Center, a Los Angeles-based organization dedicated to advancing political decentralization

00:02:55.974 --> 00:02:59.580
Based on longstanding Supreme Court precedent,

00:02:59.910 --> 00:03:05.771
The federal government cannot force a state or locality to enforce a federal law or implement a federal program.

00:03:05.965 --> 00:03:08.124
It's called the Anti-Commandeering Doctrine.

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:15.659
It's one of the last vestiges of true federalism we have in the American system. As part of the anti-commandeering doctrine.

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:21.060
The federal government cannot use funding to coerce states into doing their will.

00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:29.520
So in other words, if Brady Texas decides we're not going to enforce the vaccine mandate, the federal government can't come in and take their highway funds.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:31.020
Courts won't allow that.

00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:44.970
But the federal government could come in and say any money that we've given you that is related to coronavirus policy as a condition of that and you have to enforce these various mandates and regulations, you're not doing that.

00:03:44.970 --> 00:03:46.319
We can take the money back.

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:55.569
Nationwide, the share of each state's total revenue contributed by the federal government ranges from nearly half to a quarter, and that money generally comes with conditions.

00:03:55.590 --> 00:04:05.819
There's billions and billions of dollars that's funneled from the federal government into local police departments, both in terms of actual cash and in equipment through militarization programs.

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:10.669
Police departments love this stuff because, hey, we get to be big time and we get to have a tank.

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:17.910
And in return, the federal government gets the local law enforcement agents to be part of their war on drugs.

00:04:18.180 --> 00:04:20.389
Education is rife with federal money.

00:04:20.399 --> 00:04:35.309
We now have a national education system where minute policy matters and academic standards are dictated from the federal government, one size fits all for a country of over three hundred twenty five million people.

00:04:35.550 --> 00:04:39.759
It really doesn't make any sense, but with federal funding, it all becomes standardized.

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:45.290
It's very important for entities to realize the magnitude of transfer of sovereignty

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:53.489
entering into these contracts represent, so do not enter into contract or accept money that would infringe upon our liberties.

00:04:53.670 --> 00:04:59.760
In a lot of cases you don't know specifically what those strings are going to be because the federal

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:12.209
government writes these contracts or agreements in such a way as to give the federal government a great deal of latitude to kind of retroactively fit in what kind of policies they want in place.

00:05:12.390 --> 00:05:17.190
Brady's and Mason's City Councils  rejected the federal stimulus money by unanimous vote.

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:26.970
We had very strong support in a very short time that we had made the right choice based on what representing the citizens in this community meant.

00:05:27.420 --> 00:05:38.760
It meant giving back the funds, staying sovereign, keeping our independence, not giving the federal government a foot in our finances are over what we do with our contracts.

00:05:38.850 --> 00:05:45.029
I'm not somebody who's all for decentralization because I think state and local governments are somehow better.

00:05:45.150 --> 00:05:47.250
They're just as bad as the federal government.

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:55.449
But as with any system, a decentralized system is going to be better for the individual than a monopoly system.

00:05:55.470 --> 00:06:02.250
I'd rather deal with 50 different states than have everything one size fits all emanating from the federal government.

